Article 1 The following acts shall be acts that cause other damage to the exclusive right to use a registered trademark as specified in Item (5) of Article 52 of the Trademark Law:
(1) using a word that is identical or similar to another person's registered trademark as the name of an enterprise prominently on the same or similar goods, which is likely to cause misidentification among the relevant public;
(2) reproducing, imitating, or translating another person's well-known trademark that has been registered in China or its main part and using it as a trademark on goods that are not the same or similar, thereby misleading the public and likely causing damage to the interests of the holder of the well-known trademark;
(3) registering a word that is identical or similar to another person's registered trademark as a domain name and conducting e-commerce of related goods through that domain name, which is likely to cause misidentification among the relevant public.

Article 3 The trademark license as provided for in Article 40 of the Trademark Law shall include the following three categories:
(1) exclusive license, which means that the trademark registrant, within the agreed period, territory and in the agreed manner, licenses only one licensee to use the registered trademark, and the trademark registrant shall not use the registered trademark in accordance with the agreement;
(2) sole license, which means that the trademark registrant, within the agreed period, territory and in the agreed manner, licenses only one licensee to use the registered trademark, and the trademark registrant may use the registered trademark in accordance with the agreement but shall not license any other person to use the registered trademark;
(3) ordinary license, which means that the trademark registrant, within the agreed period, territory and in the agreed manner, licenses others to use its registered trademark, and may use the registered trademark itself and license others to use it.

Article 10 When determining whether trademarks are identical or similar in accordance with the provisions of Item (1) of Article 52 of the Trademark Law, the People's Court shall follow the following principles:
(1) taking the general attention of the relevant public as the standard;
(2) making an overall comparison of the trademarks as well as a comparison of the main parts of the trademarks, and the comparison shall be conducted separately under the condition that the objects of comparison are isolated;
(3) when determining whether the trademarks are similar, the distinctiveness and reputation of the registered trademark for which protection is sought shall be considered.
